      "id": "b0dfd919f15f66a3",
      "company": "Elastic",
      "title": "Senior Software Engineer - Storage Engine - Elasticsearch",
      "location": "Greece",
      "url": "https://jobs.elastic.co/jobs?gh_jid=8074886&gh_jid=8074886",
      "description": "<div class=\"content-intro\"><p>Elastic, the Search AI Company, enables everyone to find the answers they need in real time, using all their data, at scale \u2014 unleashing the potential of businesses and people. The Elastic Search AI Platform, used by more than 50% of the Fortune 500, brings together the precision of search and the intelligence of AI to enable everyone to accelerate the results that matter. By taking advantage of all structured and unstructured data \u2014 securing and protecting private information more effectively \u2014 Elastic\u2019s complete, cloud-based solutions for search, security, and observability help organizations deliver on the promise of AI.</p></div><h3><strong>What is The Role</strong></h3>\n<p>We're looking for a <strong>Senior Software Engineer</strong> to join the <strong>Elasticsearch - Storage Engine team</strong>.&nbsp; This globally distributed, fully remote team of experienced engineers develops core storage and query technologies for observability workloads, including improving storage efficiency, advancing ES|QL query processing and performance, and optimizing data layouts for object storage.</p>\n<p>This role is responsible for designing, implementing, and optimizing Elasticsearch's core storage and query engine. The ideal candidate has experience building high-performance, scalable database, search, or distributed systems, with a strong focus on storage, indexing, query execution, and performance optimization.</p>\n<p>Our company is distributed by intention. We hire the best engineers we can find wherever they are, whoever they are. We collaborate across continents every day over email, GitHub, Zoom, and Slack. At our best, we write fast, scalable, intuitive and high-quality software. We believe that the best way to do that is to empower individual engineers, code review every change, decide big things by consensus, and strive for incremental improvements.</p>\n<h3><strong>What You Will Be Doing</